Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API:

---

**Program.cs** (stdio transport):

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// Logging must go to stderr — stdout is the JSON-RPC protocol channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("WeatherApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weather.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

**WeatherTools.cs** (tool class wrapping a REST API):

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current weather for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetCurrentWeather(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        [Description("Units: 'metric' or 'imperial'")] string units = "metric",
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for {City}", city);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var response = await client.GetAsync(
            $"/v1/current?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&units={units}",
            cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get 5-day weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var response = await client.GetAsync(
            $"/v1/forecast?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&days=5",
            cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The MCP C# SDK integrates directly with .NET's built-in DI container:

| Pattern | How it works |
|---------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static tool classes receive services via primary constructor (as shown above). The SDK resolves the class from DI each invocation. |
| **Method parameter injection** | On static tool classes, DI services can appear as method parameters — the SDK resolves them automatically. |
| **Schema exclusion** | `CancellationToken`, `IProgress<T>`, `IMcpServer`, and any DI-registered service are **never** exposed in the tool's JSON schema. Only `[Description]`-annotated parameters become tool inputs visible to the LLM. |

**Key points:**
- Register services in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton<T>`, etc.)
- `.WithToolsFromAssembly()` discovers all `[McpServerToolType]` classes via reflection
- Each tool invocation creates a new instance (transient lifetime by default)
- `ILogger<T>` is available out of the box — no extra registration needed

To scaffold this project from the template:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
dotnet build && dotnet run
```
